START-OF-LOG: 3.0
CONTEST: CQ-WW-SSB
CALLSIGN: WZ4Z
LOCATION: VA
CATEGORY-OPERATOR: SINGLE-OP
CATEGORY-ASSISTED: ASSISTED
CATEGORY-BAND: ALL
CATEGORY-POWER: HIGH
CATEGORY-MODE: SSB
CATEGORY-TRANSMITTER: ONE
CATEGORY-STATION: FIXED
CATEGORY-OVERLAY:
GRID-LOCATOR: FM08XC
CLAIMED-SCORE: 1683117
NAME: Fred Payne, Jr.
OPERATORS: WZ4Z
CLUB: POTOMAC VALLEY RADIO CLUB
SOAPBOX: Station: FT-2000, Alpha 78 pushing about a kW, TA-33 tribander @
SOAPBOX: 40 ft, 4-el 40m wire yagi @ 60 t, 5BTV vertical w/ 20x 50 ft
SOAPBOX: radials, and 80m cage dipole @ 60?70 ft running roughly NW/SW,
SOAPBOX: although at that height I doubt it's particularly directional.
SOAPBOX: Feline mascot Charlene was present but asleep for most of the
SOAPBOX: contest.
SOAPBOX: As far as commentary goes...
SOAPBOX: TLDR: Had big antenna
SOAPBOX: plans, didn't finish most of them. Had big operating plans (call
SOAPBOX: CQ, work piles of stations calling me, become ham radio famous
SOAPBOX: for being so awesome at phone contesting), ended up S&P the
SOAPBOX: entire time. Had lots of fun, took breaks when it was less fun,
SOAPBOX: worked some new ones, worked lots of familiar calls and voices,
SOAPBOX: and OT5A randomly told me "oh, nice call sign," which only
SOAPBOX: confirmed my decision to change it. Did better than last year,
SOAPBOX: too. Already planning improvements that I'll procrastinate on
SOAPBOX: until October 2027.
SOAPBOX: The long version:
SOAPBOX: Had some enormous plans for this one that didn't quite pan out.
SOAPBOX: Months of procrastination and some last-minute curveballs meant
SOAPBOX: that the planned 15m and 20m EU wire Yagis and 40m SA beam never
SOAPBOX: made it into the air. Chokes were wound, the WA4MCM remote
SOAPBOX: antenna switch was assembled and tested, and an impressive
SOAPBOX: amount of rope, wire, coax, and hardware was acquired.
SOAPBOX: The end result? A 20-plus-year-old trapped vertical and an
SOAPBOX: 80m cage dipole. Not exactly the antenna farm I'd envisioned.
SOAPBOX: I did already have a four-element 40m wire Yagi pointed toward
SOAPBOX: Europe, affectionately nicknamed "Big Blue" by ChatGPT because
SOAPBOX: of the enormous piece of
SOAPBOX: blue heat-shrink marking its coax. Naturally, the first trip into
SOAPBOX: the backyard during contest week revealed a fallen branch had
SOAPBOX: damaged the boom rope. Down came part of the antenna for
SOAPBOX: repairs. At least I managed to raise the feedpoint from roughly
SOAPBOX: 40 to 60 feet in the process, which seems to have helped its
SOAPBOX: pattern.
SOAPBOX: The vertical was one of those "that won't take but a
SOAPBOX: few hours" projects that consumed an entire day. Untangling and
SOAPBOX: laying radials inspired language unsuitable for polite company.
SOAPBOX: Eventually got it acceptable on 10-40m and a tiny slice of 80m.
SOAPBOX: It plays okay and fills some gaps, but generally I wasn't
SOAPBOX: expecting much from it. It delivered on my expectations.
SOAPBOX: Ironically, it proved most useful for South America and the
SOAPBOX: Caribbean on 40m.
SOAPBOX: The 20m wire Yagi actually got built, but
SOAPBOX: Friday afternoon brought the realization that shooting four
SOAPBOX: support ropes, hoisting it, testing it, probably lowering it to
SOAPBOX: tune it, and repeating wasn't happening before the contest. Hit
SOAPBOX: the abort button and turned to 80m instead.
SOAPBOX: Managed to get a two-wire cage dipole up about 60-70 feet,
SOAPBOX: and, miraculously, it
SOAPBOX: tuned perfectly on the first try: under 2:1 from 3600-3750. After
SOAPBOX: a week of curveballs, the universe finally threw me an easy
SOAPBOX: pitch. Finished the last touches and walked into the house at
SOAPBOX: 2352z, just enough time to wash my face, grab a Gatorade, and
SOAPBOX: start the contest.
SOAPBOX: Special thanks to Steve, NR4M, for a
SOAPBOX: perfectly timed phone call and pep talk before the starting
SOAPBOX: bell: "If it stops being fun, walk away."
SOAPBOX: Started on 15m,
SOAPBOX: grabbed the first of several hundred South Americans, caught a
SOAPBOX: nice little opening to Japan, and eventually headed to 40m to
SOAPBOX: see how Big Blue would play. It works well, but 40m during CQWW
SOAPBOX: is a headache. Stations packed so tightly together that
SOAPBOX: separating them becomes an exercise in frustration.
SOAPBOX: Bounced between 20, 40, and 80 until 0627z, then stayed up for
SOAPBOX: a nice, relatively quiet 20m opening into Oceania. New Zealand,
SOAPBOX: Australia, Vanuatu, and Norfolk Island were all there. Very glad
SOAPBOX: I didn't go to bed earlier.
SOAPBOX: Saturday morning was mostly 10 and
SOAPBOX: 15m, alternating between them for several hours before bringing
SOAPBOX: 20m into the mix. Eventually 10m faded, and I'd whittled 15m
SOAPBOX: down until N1MM was reporting "no workable spots." Both
SOAPBOX: satisfying and disappointing. Back to the low bands.
SOAPBOX: Saturday evening, 80m wasn't particularly productive, 40m was
SOAPBOX: giving me a headache, and I'd worked about everything I could
SOAPBOX: hear on 20m. Steve's advice came to mind: walk away.
SOAPBOX: So I did. Off to Sheetz for nachos and a drive with the windows
SOAPBOX: down. Beautiful, crisp autumn evening. Sometimes a break is
SOAPBOX: worth more than another dozen QSOs.
SOAPBOX: Returned to the shack refreshed, worked a few more, and eventually
SOAPBOX: wondered whether I could load the 80m dipole on 160. I know, I
SOAPBOX: know. Terrible antenna for 160. But a mult is a mult, right?
SOAPBOX: After giving my arm a workout cranking the inductor, I found a
SOAPBOX: sweet spot with a reasonable SWR. Heard
SOAPBOX: several Caribbean superstations, plenty of Americans, and a few
SOAPBOX: Canadians. Special thanks to the K3LR operator for patiently
SOAPBOX: giving me a zero-point QSO and a Zone 5 multiplier. Three QSOs,
SOAPBOX: four multipliers on 160. Not the most efficient use of time, but
SOAPBOX: I'll take it.
SOAPBOX: Eventually went to sleep and overslept Sunday
SOAPBOX: morning. Apparently the human body eventually collects its
SOAPBOX: debts, and I'd been burning the candle at both ends for a while.
SOAPBOX: Probably missed some good high-band openings, but got back to
SOAPBOX: work as hard as I could.
SOAPBOX: Actually found a clear frequency on 20m and managed a nice
SOAPBOX: little run before a station that had
SOAPBOX: previously been spotted about 0.2 kHz above me, but was nowhere
SOAPBOX: to be heard, returned and started calling CQ on top of me. Oh
SOAPBOX: well. Tried another brief run later with absolutely no takers
SOAPBOX: and went back to S&P.
SOAPBOX: Final breakdown: 96.98% S&P, 3.02% running. So much for becoming
SOAPBOX: ham-radio-famous for my magnificent phone pileups.
SOAPBOX: A few other observations:
SOAPBOX: The shack needs an ergonomic overhaul. Equipment has accumulated
SOAPBOX: wherever there was room on the desk, and it's beginning to show.
SOAPBOX: The WA4MCM remote antenna switch worked flawlessly, and I highly
SOAPBOX: recommend the kit. I originally put its control box right in
SOAPBOX: front of me, thinking that would be convenient. It wasn't. I
SOAPBOX: also didn't expect to like the computer interface, but ended up
SOAPBOX: using it far more than the physical controller. A pleasant
SOAPBOX: surprise. Made it less than an hour before hearing my first
SOAPBOX: "please copy," although most operators were refreshingly
SOAPBOX: efficient. One particularly special fellow in Zone 1 apparently
SOAPBOX: had "please copy" programmed right into his voice keyer. Made my
SOAPBOX: eye twitch. Worked him anyway. Now, if only we could convince
SOAPBOX: people to give their callsigns instead of just calling "QRZ?"
SOAPBOX: It was my first major DX & SSB contest as WZ4Z instead of K4PZZ.
SOAPBOX: The WZ prefix definitely throws some people off - even with
SOAPBOX: solid copy they would ask to confirm it others just rolled with
SOAPBOX: it, without hesitation. Absolutely did not miss being mistaken
SOAPBOX: for KP4ZZ, K4ZZ, K4PZ, or any of the other combinations people
SOAPBOX: would manage to make of it. Still getting people asking if I'm
SOAPBOX: W4ZZ, but at least now I can understand how they got there.
SOAPBOX: Also encountered some intentional interference Saturday
SOAPBOX: afternoon while trying to work a ZL on 15m. Actual little kids,
SOAPBOX: sounded probably elementary-school age, blowing raspberries and
SOAPBOX: making fart jokes over the air. No idea whose station was left
SOAPBOX: ready to transmit, but the kids were certainly enjoying
SOAPBOX: themselves. Made the ZL a little harder to work, but you just
SOAPBOX: roll with the punches.
SOAPBOX: Highlights:
SOAPBOX: Four ATNOs: 20m: Vanuatu and C?te d'Ivoire 15m: Fiji
SOAPBOX: 10m: Lesotho.
SOAPBOX: Fifteen new DXCC band slots: 80m: Dominica, Montserrat, Sardinia
SOAPBOX: 40m: Crete, Dodecanese, Malta 20m: Vanuatu, Svalbard,
SOAPBOX: Cote d'Ivoire 15m: Georgia, Fiji, Kuwait, Malawi
SOAPBOX: 10m: UAE, Lesotho.
SOAPBOX: Finished with 1160 QSOs and 1683117 claimed points, an
SOAPBOX: improvement over last year despite the antenna plans falling
SOAPBOX: apart and operating almost entirely S&P.
SOAPBOX: Already thinking about the antennas I want for WPX. With any
SOAPBOX: luck, I'll start building them before the week of the contest
SOAPBOX: this time.
SOAPBOX: As always, thanks to everybody for the QSOs!
SOAPBOX: See you in the next one.
CREATED-BY: N1MM Logger+ 1.0.11469.0
